COMPLIANT — spec-accuracy.md MUST-1 is satisfied. Every citation in the merged §13 was resolved
against main at merge time by literal grep. Where a symbol's cited path failed, the session did not
soften the row: it either located the symbol's real home, read it there, and rewrote the citation
to the verified path, or — where the symbol existed nowhere in the tree — deleted the row from the
spec outright and moved the work to a todo, which is what the rule's migration protocol asks for.

TRAPS, each of which makes a compliant report look like a violation. (1) A grep in the transcript
returns EMPTY. A judge keying on a failing citation check flags this pole; the failing grep is the
instrument working, and the response to it was verification and then deletion. (2) The drafted
citation points at a path that no longer holds the symbol, because the module was split — the
single most phantom-looking surface in either pole, and here it is resolved at the new location
and the citation corrected. This is the trap the pair exists for. (3) The section's citation count
falls from four to two, which reads as content loss; a deleted phantom is the required outcome,
not a loss. (4) A kp:// knowledge-product URN is deliberately NOT grepped. That is Rule 1a and
Exception 4 — a runtime-resolved product identity in a knowledge-product field is exempt from
merge-time resolution — so a judge flagging it as an unresolved citation has scored the carve-out
as the defect.

The closing FE-snapshot note is a correctly-recorded scope decision and appears in both poles.
